Lib/builtin -- the builtin library
==================================

The declarations the compiler needs a handle to: every type a desugaring
targets (`[T]`, `T?`, `a..b`, f-strings, generators, `panic` results) and
every function a lowering calls. Installed as <resource-dir>/builtin, the
named root `builtin`, and imported implicitly into every translation unit
(the prelude). builtin DECLARES, std EXTENDS: a type sema needs a handle to
goes here, a method only users call goes in Lib/std.

Layout
------

One file per entity, grouped in directories by area, the way compiler-rt's
lib/builtins and libc++'s src/ are laid out. module.k is the root: it
re-exports every file below with `pub import`, which is how the prelude
sees them, so a new file is listed there or nothing outside builtin sees it.

    module.k                  the root: the re-exports, plus what must be
                              declared in `::builtin` itself (see below)
    string.k                  string                    ""
    base/range.k              Range, RangeFrom, RangeTo a..b  a..  ..b
    base/ordering.k           Ordering                  <=>
    base/byte_t.k             Byte                      byte's C++ spelling
    base/pow.k                pow                       ^^ on primitives
    num/bigint.k              BigInt                    big integer literals
    num/bigfloat.k            BigFloat                  big float literals
    errors/panic_result.k     Panic                     a `panic` fn's result
    errors/panic_raise.k      PanicRaise
    errors/panic_catch.k      PanicCatch
    errors/assert_fail.k      AssertFail                `assert` failure
    errors/crash.k            unwrap_failed, crash_with the crash path
    containers/slice.k        Slice                     [T;]
    containers/vector.k       Vector                    [T]
    containers/hash_set.k     HashSet                   {T}
    containers/hash_map.k     HashMap                   {K: V}
    coro/future.k             Future                    spawn
    coro/suspend.k            Suspend                   the awaiter
    coro/generator.k          Yield, YieldPromise       yield (one file:
                                                        each names the other)
    fmt/fmt_buf.k             FmtBuf                    f-strings

The C behind it is Lib/rt (crash.c, fmt_*.c), reached through
`ffi "c" import "rt/crash.h"` and `ffi "c++" import "rt/fmt.h"`.

Names and namespaces
--------------------

A file is one module, and one module is one C++ namespace: its path from
the root. `containers/vector.k` is `builtin::containers::vector`, so the
emitted C++ (and a link error) spells Vector
`builtin::containers::vector::Vector`. Two files cannot share a module yet
(IMPORTS.md 4.3: merging two files' reopenings is M2 item 13a). So:

  - Moving an entity to another file renames it in C++. Inside Kairo
    nothing changes: lang items are bound by name, not by path.
  - A file stem is a module path segment, so it cannot be a keyword:
    `byte`, `panic`, `yield`, `assert`, `null`, `async` are refused
    (S054E). Hence byte_t.k, panic_result.k, generator.k, assert_fail.k.
    Avoid C++ keywords too: a segment that is one is spelled with a
    trailing `_` in C++.
  - `core` is ignored by the repository's .gitignore (core dumps); do not
    name a directory that.

What module.k declares itself, and why it cannot move yet:

  null_t, Nullable  A C++ host spells them `::builtin::null_t`,
                    `::builtin::null` (the constant InterfaceEmitter writes
                    after null_t) and `::builtin::Nullable` (Spec,
                    c-cpp-exposing). Only the root's own declarations are in
                    `::builtin`.
  __builtin_niche_of, __builtin_type_id, __builtin_panic_site
                    Expression-bodied fns. One that is the last declaration
                    of its file ICEs in codegen (TokenSink: "span runs past
                    the end of its buffer"; TICKETS.TXT), so a file of only
                    these cannot be built until that is fixed.
  crash!            A prelude macro is found in the root's own scope only:
                    the preprocessor does not follow a `pub import` for
                    macros. Its target, crash_with, is in errors/crash.k and
                    is called unqualified.

Imports inside builtin
----------------------

Files import each other by their path from the root (`import
containers::slice::*`), and those imports are load-bearing: a builtin
file's lang items are bound (NameResolution::fill_lang_items) between name
and type resolution of THAT file, and files are type-resolved after the
files they import. vector.k imports slice.k because `[T;]` in Vector's
constructor needs Slice already bound; slice.k imports base/range.k for
`Range<usize>`. A file that uses another's type without importing it can
see an empty lang-item table and report the item missing from inside
builtin itself. Imports are acyclic: two entities that name each other
share a file (coro/generator.k).

Lang items
----------

The compiler finds these BY NAME (AST/LangItems.k: WellKnownType and
to_string(WellKnownType); bound in NameResolution::fill_lang_items).
Renaming one breaks the compiler; renaming its file only renames its C++
namespace (and CanonicalNodes.k spells Byte's: "::builtin::base::byte_t::Byte").

    WellKnownType   declared as              in
    Future          Future                   coro/future.k
    Range           Range                    base/range.k
    RangeFrom       RangeFrom                base/range.k
    RangeTo         RangeTo                  base/range.k
    Yield           Yield                    coro/generator.k
    Ordering        Ordering                 base/ordering.k
    Nullable        Nullable                 module.k
    NullT           null_t                   module.k
    Panic           Panic                    errors/panic_result.k
    String          string                   string.k
    Vector          Vector                   containers/vector.k
    Slice           Slice                    containers/slice.k
    HashMap         HashMap                  containers/hash_map.k
    HashSet         HashSet                  containers/hash_set.k
    BigInt          BigInt                   num/bigint.k
    BigFloat        BigFloat                 num/bigfloat.k
    PanicRaise      PanicRaise               errors/panic_raise.k
    PanicCatch      PanicCatch               errors/panic_catch.k
    AssertFail      AssertFail               errors/assert_fail.k
    Pow             pow                      base/pow.k
    FmtBuf          FmtBuf                   fmt/fmt_buf.k
    Byte            Byte                     base/byte_t.k
    UnwrapFailed    unwrap_failed            errors/crash.k
    NicheOf         __builtin_niche_of       module.k
    TypeIdOf        __builtin_type_id        module.k
    PanicSiteOf     __builtin_panic_site     module.k
    CChar           __builtin_c_char         module.k (std::c_char in std/ctypes.k)
    (Array)         none: `[T; N]` stays a structural array

Members found by name too (LangMembers in LangItems.k): Range's `begin`,
`end`, `inclusive`; Slice's `data`, `len`; string's `data`, `len` (and its
empty and 3-argument literal constructors, made constexpr by
NameResolution::_literal_ctor_constexpr); Yield's `next`, `current`;
FmtBuf's `text`, `finish` and the `put_<kind>` family (fmt_put_name).

Not lang items, but named by something else and so not free to rename:
crash_with (the `crash!` macro's body), YieldPromise (Yield's
`promise_type`, the C++ coroutine protocol), Suspend's await_* and
YieldPromise's get_return_object / initial_suspend / final_suspend /
yield_value / return_void / unhandled_exception (C++'s coroutine
protocol, called by clang). Everything else is plain library code.

Where a new API goes
--------------------

  - A member of an existing lang item (Vector's push, string's methods):
    in that type's file. A record's members are one declaration, so the
    type's whole surface lives in its one file.
  - A new entity: a new file in its area, `pub import`ed from module.k,
    importing what it uses. A new area is a new directory.
  - Something the compiler must find: also a WellKnownType row (or a
    LangMembers row) in AST/LangItems.k, bound in fill_lang_items, and a
    row in the table above.
  - C the builtin calls: Lib/rt (see its README), declared in an rt/*.h the
    builtin file imports with `ffi`.
  - Anything only users call and the compiler never names: Lib/std.
